    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has identified the key idea – the commutator reverses the current. I now confirm the precise function, link it to continuous rotation, and give a concise formula breakdown and exam tip as required.",
      "content": "Yes, the commutator reverses the direction of the current in the coil each half‑turn, which allows the coil to keep turning in the same direction.\n\n**Final Answer:** Ensures continuous rotation of the coil.\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $I = \\frac{V}{R}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $I$: electric current (how much charge flows per second)\n  * $V$: voltage supplied to the coil (the electrical “push”)\n  * $R$: resistance of the coil (how much the coil opposes the flow)\n* **Why we use it:** The commutator switches the connections so that, even though the voltage polarity stays the same, the direction of current $I$ through the coil reverses each half‑turn. This reversal keeps the magnetic forces acting in the same rotational direction, producing continuous motion.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In motor questions, allocate marks for describing *both* the mechanical effect (continuous rotation) and the electrical effect (current reversal). Forgetting one can lose you a mark."
    }
  ],
