      "content": "You've got two of them: tension ($T$) and weight ($F_g$). The other two are the normal force ($F_N$) from the water pushing up, and the frictional force ($f$) from the water opposing the horizontal motion. So the free-body diagram has four forces:\n- $T$ (tension) acting horizontally in the direction of the helicopter's motion.\n- $f$ (friction) acting horizontally opposite to the motion.\n- $F_N$ (normal force) acting vertically upwards.\n- $F_g$ (weight) acting vertically downwards.\n\n**Final Answer:** Free-body diagram showing Tension ($T$), Normal force ($F_N$), Frictional force ($f$), and Weight ($F_g$).\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\sum F = 0$ (since the helicopter moves at constant speed, the net force is zero).\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $T$: Tension force from the rope pulling the tube forward.\n  * $f$: Frictional force from the water resisting the forward motion.\n  * $F_N$: Normal force from the water pushing up on the tube.\n  * $F_g$: Weight (gravitational force) pulling the man-tube system downwards.\n* **Why we use it:** A free-body diagram helps us see all forces acting on an object. Since the speed is constant, the forward forces balance the backward forces, and the upward forces balance the downward forces.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always check that you have included all forces: contact forces (tension, friction, normal) and non-contact forces (weight). Don't forget friction even if the motion is at constant speed!"
